1:["$","div",null,{"className":"page","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"page-title","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"A mathematical companion"}],["$","h1",null,{"children":"A negative solution to the complemented subspace problem for Banach spaces with unconditional bases"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Antonio Acuaviva"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"results-introduction","children":"This website accompanies the paper and presents the formal verification of its principal results in Lean. Each mathematical statement appears alongside its Lean formulation, with expandable, step-by-step explanations."}],[["$","section",null,{"className":"result","id":"theorem-a","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Theorem A"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"result-grid","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"result-mathematics","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"panel-label","children":"Mathematical statement · real field"}],["$","$Le",null,{"text":"For every $\\rho>0$, there exist sequences $(N_j)_{j=1}^{\\infty}$ in $\\mathbb N$ and $(p_j)_{j=1}^{\\infty}$ in $(2,3]$, with $p_j\\downarrow2$, and a projection $P_\\rho$ on the separable superreflexive real Banach lattice\n\\[\nX_\\rho=\\left(\\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\ell_{p_j}^{N_j}(\\mathbb R)\\right)_2\n\\]\nsuch that, with $Z_\\rho=P_\\rho(X_\\rho)$:\n(i) for $V\\in\\{Z_\\rho,Z_\\rho^*\\}$,\n\\[\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{GL}}(V)\\leq\\|P_\\rho\\|<1+\\rho,\\qquad\\chi_{\\mathrm{DPR}}(V)=\\infty;\n\\]\n(ii) $P_\\rho$ can be chosen so that $\\|I-P_\\rho\\|<1+\\rho$ and (i) also holds for $(I-P_\\rho)(X_\\rho)$ and its dual, with $P_\\rho$ replaced by $I-P_\\rho$."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note correspondence-note","children":"The Lean statement requires uniform convexity of the displayed norm, which implies superreflexivity. The manuscript also establishes uniform convexity in Corollary 4.2(ii). The exponents are nonincreasing, and Lean starts the block index at 0."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"data-statement":"RealMainTheoremStatement","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"def RealMainTheoremStatement : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ →\n    ∃ a : BlockParameters,\n      TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace (Ambient a) ∧\n      UniformConvexSpace (Ambient a) ∧\n      HasRealBanachLatticeOrder (Ambient a) ∧\n      ∃ P : Ambient a →L[ℝ] Ambient a,\n        P.comp P = P ∧\n        ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n        ‖ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) - P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n        HasSeparatedRange P ∧\n        HasSeparatedRange (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) - P)","label":"Exact proposition definition · complete body"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"source-line","children":["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/TheoremStatement.lean","line":55,"children":"Statement source"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"id":"realMainTheorem","className":"proved-reference","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"theorem realMainTheorem : RealMainTheoremStatement","label":"Proved declaration · proof body omitted"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"verification-line","children":["Verified in Lean ·"," ",["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/RealMainTheorem.lean","line":15,"children":"Source"}]," ","· ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/verification/","children":"Verification details"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note","children":["The definition states the result; ",["$","code",null,{"children":"realMainTheorem"}]," ","proves it."]}]]}]]}],["$","$L11",null,{"guideId":"RealMainTheoremStatement","code":"def RealMainTheoremStatement : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ →\n    ∃ a : BlockParameters,\n      TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace (Ambient a) ∧\n      UniformConvexSpace (Ambient a) ∧\n      HasRealBanachLatticeOrder (Ambient a) ∧\n      ∃ P : Ambient a →L[ℝ] Ambient a,\n        P.comp P = P ∧\n        ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n        ‖ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) - P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n        HasSeparatedRange P ∧\n        HasSeparatedRange (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) - P)","steps":[{"fragment":"def RealMainTheoremStatement : Prop :=","syntax":"def introduces a definition. RealMainTheoremStatement is its name; : Prop says its value is a proposition, and := begins that proposition.","math":"We name the following statement RealMainTheoremStatement.","role":"This is the statement corresponding to Theorem A, with the stronger uniform-convexity clause below. Defining it does not prove it; the closed declaration realMainTheorem proves this proposition."},{"fragment":"∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ →","syntax":"∀ means “for every”; ρ : ℝ makes ρ a real number. Here → means implication: under the hypothesis 0 < ρ, everything to the right must hold.","math":"For every real $\\rho>0$, the following witnesses and conclusions exist.","role":"This is the opening “For every positive tolerance” of Theorem A. All subsequent choices may depend on this ρ; the same ρ is used in both norm bounds."},{"fragment":"∃ a : BlockParameters,","syntax":"∃ means “there exists”. Here a specifies the two sequences in BlockParameters, satisfying all its conditions.","math":"Choose positive integers $N_j$ and real exponents $2<p_j\\leq3$, with $p_{j+1}\\leq p_j$ and $p_j\\to2$.","role":"These are the sequences in Theorem A. Ambient a is the corresponding Banach space. Lean indexes the sequences from 0. Nonincreasing does not mean strictly decreasing.","terms":["BlockParameters","Ambient"]},{"fragment":"TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace (Ambient a) ∧","syntax":"Ambient a applies the space construction Ambient to the chosen parameters a. The parentheses make it the argument of SeparableSpace. The final ∧ means “and”, joining this clause to all the following clauses.","math":"The space $X=(\\bigoplus_j\\ell_{p_j}^{N_j}(\\mathbb R))_2$ is separable: it has a countable dense subset.","role":"This requires the ambient space in Theorem A to be separable. Every later occurrence of Ambient a refers to this same choice of a.","terms":["TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace","Ambient","Block"]},{"fragment":"UniformConvexSpace (Ambient a) ∧","syntax":"UniformConvexSpace means that the space is uniformly convex. Again ∧ means that this and the remaining conditions hold together.","math":"The existing norm of the same space $X$ is uniformly convex.","role":"The literal clause strengthens the displayed superreflexivity conclusion of Theorem A; the manuscript also establishes uniform convexity in Corollary 4.2(ii). The mathematical consequence “hence superreflexive” is distinct from what this Lean clause literally says.","terms":["UniformConvexSpace","Ambient"]},{"fragment":"HasRealBanachLatticeOrder (Ambient a) ∧","syntax":"The project-defined predicate HasRealBanachLatticeOrder is applied to that same Ambient a. Its definition existentially chooses a lattice order; ∧ continues the conjunction.","math":"The space $X$, with its existing complete norm, admits a real vector-lattice order compatible with addition and nonnegative scalar multiplication, and the norm is solid: $|x|\\leq|y|$ implies $\\|x\\|\\leq\\|y\\|$.","role":"This is the real Banach lattice condition in Theorem A. It chooses an order on X without replacing its norm or its scalar structure.","terms":["HasRealBanachLatticeOrder","Ambient"]},{"fragment":"∃ P : Ambient a →L[ℝ] Ambient a,","syntax":"∃ P chooses a map. In X →L[ℝ] X, the spaces before and after the arrow are its domain and codomain; L and [ℝ] specify a continuous real-linear map.","math":"There exists a bounded real-linear operator $P:X\\to X$.","role":"This chooses the single operator used in every remaining clause. It introduces an operator; the next clause establishes that it is a projection.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap","Ambient"]},{"fragment":"P.comp P = P ∧","syntax":"P.comp P is the composition of P with itself. = means equality of operators, and ∧ keeps the remaining requirements attached to the same P.","math":"$$P^2=P$, so $P$ is a projection.","role":"This is the projection requirement in Theorem A, not an additional choice of operator.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧","syntax":"The norm bars denote the operator norm of the bounded linear map P. < is a strict inequality, and ρ is the positive number fixed at the beginning.","math":"$$\\|P\\|<1+\\rho$.","role":"This is the upper projection-norm bound in Theorem A(i); it constrains the P already chosen. The theorem does not choose a new tolerance here."},{"fragment":"‖ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) - P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧","syntax":"ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) is the identity real-linear operator on X. Subtracting P forms another bounded operator; the surrounding norm bars take the norm of this entire difference.","math":"$$\\|I_X-P\\|<1+\\rho$. Since $P^2=P$, $I_X-P$ is the complementary projection.","role":"This is the first additional requirement in Theorem A(ii). It holds for the same P and the same ρ as the preceding bound.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap.id","ContinuousLinearMap","Ambient"]},{"fragment":"HasSeparatedRange P ∧","syntax":"Applying HasSeparatedRange to P substitutes P into a four-clause project definition. In that definition P.range is its range, →L[ℝ] ℝ is the continuous real dual, ⊤ is infinity, and ‖P‖ₑ is the operator norm in the extended nonnegative reals.","math":"Put $Z=P(X)$. For both $V=Z$ and $V=Z^*$, $\\chi_{\\mathrm{GL}}(V)\\leq\\|P\\|$ and $\\chi_{\\mathrm{DPR}}(V)=\\infty$. The range carries the inherited norm and the continuous dual carries its operator norm.","role":"These are the four local-structure conclusions in Theorem A(i). Together with the earlier inequality they give the displayed chain χGL(V) ≤ ||P|| < 1+ρ. Idempotence is a separate requirement.","terms":["HasSeparatedRange","chiGL","chiDPR","ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"HasSeparatedRange (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ (Ambient a) - P)","syntax":"The parentheses give the whole operator I_X - P as the argument of the same predicate. Its definition therefore uses the range and norm of I_X - P throughout.","math":"Put $W=(I_X-P)(X)$. For both $V=W$ and $V=W^*$, $\\chi_{\\mathrm{GL}}(V)\\leq\\|I_X-P\\|$ and $\\chi_{\\mathrm{DPR}}(V)=\\infty$.","role":"This completes Theorem A(ii). Both complementary ranges and both continuous duals satisfy separation simultaneously, for the one chosen X and P. The complementary GL bound is ||I_X-P||, not ||P||.","terms":["HasSeparatedRange","ContinuousLinearMap.id","chiGL","chiDPR"]}],"children":"$L12"}]]}],"$L13","$L14"]]}]
12:["$","p",null,{"className":"assembled-reading","children":"Taken together: for every positive tolerance, choose one block space and one projection. That space has all three geometric properties, both complementary projection norms satisfy the strict bounds, and both ranges and both continuous duals satisfy the GL/DPR conclusions."}]
13:["$","section",null,{"className":"result","id":"theorem-b","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Theorem B"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"result-grid","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"result-mathematics","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"panel-label","children":"Mathematical statement · real and complex fields"}],["$","$Le",null,{"text":"For $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and every $\\rho>0$, there are a Banach space $X_\\rho$ over $\\mathbb K$ with a $1$-unconditional basis and a projection $P_\\rho$ on $X_\\rho$ with $\\|P_\\rho\\|<1+\\rho$ such that, writing $Z_\\rho=P_\\rho(X_\\rho)$, neither $Z_\\rho$ nor $Z_\\rho^*$ admits an unconditional basis. If $\\mathbb K=\\mathbb R$, neither space is isomorphic to a Banach lattice."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note correspondence-note","children":"The real declaration on the right includes the two nonisomorphism conclusions. The common basis conclusion and its complex specialisation follow below."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"scalar-heading","children":"Real case"}],["$","div",null,{"data-statement":"RealCorollaryStatement","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"def RealCorollaryStatement : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ → ∃ X : BanachModel.{0} ℝ,\n    HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ X.Carrier ∧\n    ∃ P : X.Carrier →L[ℝ] X.Carrier,\n      P.comp P = P ∧ ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ P.range ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ (P.range →L[ℝ] ℝ) ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice P.range ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice (P.range →L[ℝ] ℝ)","label":"Exact proposition definition · complete body"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"source-line","children":["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/CorollaryStatement.lean","line":82,"children":"Statement source"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"id":"realCorollary","className":"proved-reference","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"theorem realCorollary : RealCorollaryStatement","label":"Proved declaration · proof body omitted"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"verification-line","children":["Verified in Lean ·"," ",["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/RealMainConsequences.lean","line":22,"children":"Source"}]," ","· ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/verification/","children":"Verification details"}]]}]]}]]}]]}],["$","$L11",null,{"guideId":"RealCorollaryStatement","code":"def RealCorollaryStatement : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ → ∃ X : BanachModel.{0} ℝ,\n    HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ X.Carrier ∧\n    ∃ P : X.Carrier →L[ℝ] X.Carrier,\n      P.comp P = P ∧ ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ P.range ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ (P.range →L[ℝ] ℝ) ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice P.range ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice (P.range →L[ℝ] ℝ)","steps":[{"fragment":"def RealCorollaryStatement : Prop :=","syntax":"def names a definition; : Prop says it is a proposition, and := introduces its full content.","math":"We name the real-field statement RealCorollaryStatement.","role":"This proposition expresses the real case of Theorem B, including both nonlattice conclusions. The separate closed theorem realCorollary proves it."},{"fragment":"∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ →","syntax":"For every real number ρ, assume 0 < ρ. The implication arrow makes every subsequent existential and conjunction part of the conclusion under that assumption.","math":"For every $\\rho>0$, the following holds.","role":"The tolerance is arbitrary. The witnesses chosen next may depend on it."},{"fragment":"∃ X : BanachModel.{0} ℝ,","syntax":"∃ chooses a real Banach space X. X.Carrier is its underlying space; .{0} concerns the size of the type, not its vector-space dimension.","math":"There exists a real Banach space $X$.","role":"This chooses the ambient space in the real case of Theorem B. All later basis and projection clauses refer to this one witness.","terms":["BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ X.Carrier ∧","syntax":"The predicate takes the field ℝ and the underlying space X.Carrier as arguments. ∧ means this condition and the following ones must all hold.","math":"$$X$ has an $\\mathbb N$-indexed $1$-unconditional Schauder basis: every finite coordinate multiplier with real coefficients of modulus at most one is contractive.","role":"This is the ambient basis in Theorem B. It is a basis with continuous coordinates and norm convergence, rather than only finite-dimensional local structure.","terms":["HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis","IsOneUnconditional","BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"∃ P : X.Carrier →L[ℝ] X.Carrier,","syntax":"∃ P chooses a continuous real-linear endomorphism of the same underlying space X.Carrier. On normed spaces, such linear maps are bounded operators.","math":"There exists a bounded real-linear map $P:X\\to X$.","role":"The remaining clauses constrain this one P and its one range; the next clause makes it a projection.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap","BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"P.comp P = P ∧","syntax":"comp denotes operator composition. The equality requires idempotence, and ∧ attaches all the remaining requirements to the chosen operator.","math":"$$P^2=P$.","role":"This is “a projection” in the real case of Theorem B.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧","syntax":"‖P‖ is its operator norm; < makes the upper bound strict. The same positive ρ introduced above appears here.","math":"$$\\|P\\|<1+\\rho$.","role":"This is the quantitative projection bound in Theorem B. This statement includes no norm bound for I-P."},{"fragment":"¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ P.range ∧","syntax":"¬ negates the whole basis-existence predicate. P.range denotes the range subspace, used with its inherited norm. ∧ continues the conjunction.","math":"The range $Z=P(X)$ admits no unconditional Schauder basis over $\\mathbb R$, whether finite or indexed by $\\mathbb N$.","role":"This is the first negative basis conclusion of Theorem B. The predicate includes the empty basis of the zero space; this exclusion has no finite-dimensional loophole.","terms":["HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis","Submodule"]},{"fragment":"¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ (P.range →L[ℝ] ℝ) ∧","syntax":"The parenthesized type consists of continuous real-linear functionals on P.range. ¬ again denies existence of either kind of unconditional Schauder basis.","math":"The continuous real dual $Z^*=\\mathcal B_{\\mathbb R}(Z,\\mathbb R)$, with its operator norm, admits no unconditional Schauder basis.","role":"This is the second negative basis conclusion, for the dual of the same range Z. It is not a claim about the algebraic dual.","terms":["HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis","ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice P.range ∧","syntax":"The negated project predicate would choose some complete real Banach-space model with a compatible lattice order and a bounded real-linear isomorphism from P.range to that model.","math":"$$Z=P(X)$ is not isomorphic, as a real Banach space, to any real Banach lattice.","role":"This is the first extra conclusion for the real field in Theorem B. It excludes all target lattice norms, not only lattice orders on Z with its present norm; the isomorphism need not preserve order.","terms":["IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice","HasRealBanachLatticeOrder","BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice (P.range →L[ℝ] ℝ)","syntax":"The same negated isomorphism-existence predicate is now applied to the continuous real dual of P.range.","math":"$$Z^*$ is not isomorphic, as a real Banach space, to any real Banach lattice.","role":"This completes the real case of Theorem B. All four exclusions concern the range of the single chosen projection and its continuous dual.","terms":["IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice","ContinuousLinearMap"]}],"children":"$undefined"}],"$L15","$L16","$L17","$L18"]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"result","id":"corollary-c","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Corollary C"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"result-grid","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"result-mathematics","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"panel-label","children":"Mathematical statement"}],["$","$Le",null,{"text":"The class of separable real Banach lattices is not primary."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note correspondence-note","children":"The restriction to real scalars and the counterexample are given in §4.3 and Corollary 4.5 of the manuscript."}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"The counterexample in Lean"}],["$","$Le",null,{"text":"For every $\\rho>0$ there is a separable real Banach lattice $X$, isomorphic to a complete uniformly convex space, with a $1$-unconditional Schauder basis and a bounded real-linear map $P:X\\to X$ such that $P^2=P$, $\\|P\\|<1+\\rho$, $\\|I-P\\|<1+\\rho$, and neither $P(X)$ nor $(I-P)(X)$ is isomorphic to any real Banach lattice."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note correspondence-note","children":"The Lean statement describes the counterexample: neither complementary summand is isomorphic to a Banach lattice. It does not introduce a general predicate for a primary class. Here superreflexivity means isomorphism to a complete uniformly convex space."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"data-statement":"SeparableNonprimarityStatement","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"def SeparableNonprimarityStatement : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ → ∃ X : BanachModel.{0} ℝ,\n    TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace X.Carrier ∧\n    HasRealBanachLatticeOrder X.Carrier ∧\n    IsSuperreflexiveByRenorming ℝ X.Carrier ∧\n    HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ X.Carrier ∧\n    ∃ P : X.Carrier →L[ℝ] X.Carrier,\n      P.comp P = P ∧ ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ‖ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier - P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice P.range ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice\n        (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier - P).range","label":"Exact proposition definition · complete body"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"source-line","children":["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/CorollaryStatement.lean","line":95,"children":"Statement source"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"id":"realSeparableNonprimarity","className":"proved-reference","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"theorem realSeparableNonprimarity : SeparableNonprimarityStatement","label":"Proved declaration · proof body omitted"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"verification-line","children":["Verified in Lean ·"," ",["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/RealMainConsequences.lean","line":54,"children":"Source"}]," ","· ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/verification/","children":"Verification details"}]]}]]}]]}]]}],["$","$L11",null,{"guideId":"SeparableNonprimarityStatement","code":"def SeparableNonprimarityStatement : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ → ∃ X : BanachModel.{0} ℝ,\n    TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace X.Carrier ∧\n    HasRealBanachLatticeOrder X.Carrier ∧\n    IsSuperreflexiveByRenorming ℝ X.Carrier ∧\n    HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ X.Carrier ∧\n    ∃ P : X.Carrier →L[ℝ] X.Carrier,\n      P.comp P = P ∧ ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ‖ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier - P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice P.range ∧\n      ¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice\n        (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier - P).range","steps":[{"fragment":"def SeparableNonprimarityStatement : Prop :=","syntax":"def names a proposition, : Prop identifies it as a proposition, and := begins its content.","math":"We name the following statement of a counterexample to primariness.","role":"The theorem realSeparableNonprimarity proves this proposition. It provides a concrete counterexample for Corollary C rather than defining a general “primary class” predicate."},{"fragment":"∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ →","syntax":"∀ ranges over real ρ. Under the positivity hypothesis, the implication requires all the following witnesses and properties.","math":"For every $\\rho>0$, the following holds.","role":"The quantitative witness can be chosen for every positive tolerance. Both projection bounds below use this same ρ."},{"fragment":"∃ X : BanachModel.{0} ℝ,","syntax":"∃ chooses a real Banach space. X.Carrier is its underlying space. The .{0} annotation fixes the type universe; it does not mean that the dimension is zero.","math":"There exists a real Banach space $X$.","role":"This is the single ambient space of the counterexample. It may depend on ρ, and all the following properties concern this same X.","terms":["BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace X.Carrier ∧","syntax":"The library separability predicate is applied to the underlying space X. The final ∧ requires this and the remaining clauses together.","math":"$$X$ is separable: it has a countable dense subset.","role":"This puts the ambient example in the separable setting of Corollary C.","terms":["TopologicalSpace.SeparableSpace","BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"HasRealBanachLatticeOrder X.Carrier ∧","syntax":"This project predicate chooses a compatible real lattice order on the existing complete normed space X.Carrier. It does not choose a new ambient norm.","math":"$$X$ is a real Banach lattice in its given norm: the order respects the vector-space operations and the norm is solid.","role":"This places the ambient example in the class whose primariness is disproved. The two summands will fail membership even up to isomorphism.","terms":["HasRealBanachLatticeOrder"]},{"fragment":"IsSuperreflexiveByRenorming ℝ X.Carrier ∧","syntax":"The project predicate takes the field ℝ and X.Carrier. Its definition chooses a complete uniformly convex normed real space H and a continuous linear equivalence between X and H.","math":"$$X$ is isomorphic to a complete uniformly convex real normed space; equivalently, it has an equivalent uniformly convex norm.","role":"This is the superreflexivity convention used for the quantitative Corollary C witness. It is a renormability condition, distinct from a literal UniformConvexSpace X clause.","terms":["IsSuperreflexiveByRenorming","UniformConvexSpace"]},{"fragment":"HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis ℝ X.Carrier ∧","syntax":"The project predicate specifies an ℕ-indexed real unconditional Schauder basis and requires all finite coordinate multipliers of modulus at most one to be contractions.","math":"The ambient space $X$ has a $1$-unconditional Schauder basis.","role":"This adds the ambient basis promised by the explicit witness in Corollary 4.5, p.22. It is compatible with all the preceding conditions on the same X.","terms":["HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis","IsOneUnconditional"]},{"fragment":"∃ P : X.Carrier →L[ℝ] X.Carrier,","syntax":"∃ P chooses a continuous real-linear operator on the underlying space X, which is the same real Banach space fixed above.","math":"There exists a bounded real-linear map $P:X\\to X$.","role":"The following idempotence, two norm inequalities and two negations all concern this single P.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap","BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"P.comp P = P ∧","syntax":"comp means operator composition. This equality makes P idempotent; ∧ continues the conjunction.","math":"$$P^2=P$. Hence $P$ and $I_X-P$ are complementary projections and $X=P(X)\\oplus(I_X-P)(X)$.","role":"This gives the complemented decomposition used to refute primariness. Because P is bounded, both summands are closed.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧","syntax":"Take the operator norm of P and compare it strictly with 1 + ρ, using the tolerance chosen at the start.","math":"$$\\|P\\|<1+\\rho$.","role":"This controls the first projection in the quantitative nonprimarity witness."},{"fragment":"‖ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier - P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧","syntax":"ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier is I_X. Subtraction forms I_X - P before its operator norm is taken. The same ρ appears in the strict upper bound.","math":"$$\\|I_X-P\\|<1+\\rho$.","role":"This controls the complementary projection simultaneously; no new operator or tolerance is chosen.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap.id","ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice P.range ∧","syntax":"P.range is the first range with its inherited norm. ¬ negates existence of any complete real Banach lattice model and a bounded real-linear isomorphism from this range to that model.","math":"$$P(X)$ is not isomorphic to any real Banach lattice.","role":"This excludes the first summand from the lattice class, even after a change of norm. The isomorphism in the negated predicate is a Banach-space isomorphism; it need not preserve order.","terms":["IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice","HasRealBanachLatticeOrder","Submodule"]},{"fragment":"¬ IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice\n        (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℝ X.Carrier - P).range","syntax":"The parentheses form I_X - P, and .range then takes its range. The same isomorphism-existence predicate is negated for this second subspace. The physical line break does not split the clause.","math":"$$(I_X-P)(X)$ is not isomorphic to any real Banach lattice.","role":"Together with the preceding exclusion, this gives a separable real Banach lattice decomposed into two closed summands, neither isomorphic to a Banach lattice: the counterexample in Corollary C. This exact statement does not add nonlattice conclusions for the summands’ duals.","terms":["IsIsomorphicToRealBanachLattice","ContinuousLinearMap.id","Submodule"]}],"children":"$undefined"}]]}]
15:["$","div",null,{"className":"result-grid scalar-pair","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"result-mathematics","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Basis conclusion over each field"}],["$","$Le",null,{"text":"This proposition states the unconditional-basis part of Theorem B. The theorem $\\mathtt{realUnconditionalCorollary}$ proves it for $\\mathbb R$, and $\\mathtt{complexCorollary}$ proves its specialisation to $\\mathbb C$."}],["$","p",null,{"children":"The generic proposition is defined for a normed scalar field. The two proved declarations displayed here concern the real and complex fields."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"note","children":"Over ℂ, the operators and continuous dual are complex-linear. The ambient 1-unconditional basis is contractive under complex coordinate multipliers of modulus at most one, including all phases. The complex theorem proves the basis conclusion."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"data-statement":"UnconditionalCorollaryStatement","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"def UnconditionalCorollaryStatement (𝕜 : Type*) [NontriviallyNormedField 𝕜] : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ → ∃ X : BanachModel.{0} 𝕜,\n    HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 X.Carrier ∧\n    ∃ P : X.Carrier →L[𝕜] X.Carrier,\n      P.comp P = P ∧ ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 P.range ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 (P.range →L[𝕜] 𝕜)","label":"Exact proposition definition · complete body"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"source-line","children":["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/CorollaryStatement.lean","line":73,"children":"Statement source"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"id":"realUnconditionalCorollary","className":"proved-reference","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"theorem realUnconditionalCorollary : UnconditionalCorollaryStatement ℝ","label":"Proved declaration · proof body omitted"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"verification-line","children":["Verified in Lean ·"," ",["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/RealMainConsequences.lean","line":39,"children":"Source"}]," ","· ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/verification/","children":"Verification details"}]]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","$L11",null,{"guideId":"UnconditionalCorollaryStatement","code":"def UnconditionalCorollaryStatement (𝕜 : Type*) [NontriviallyNormedField 𝕜] : Prop :=\n  ∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ → ∃ X : BanachModel.{0} 𝕜,\n    HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 X.Carrier ∧\n    ∃ P : X.Carrier →L[𝕜] X.Carrier,\n      P.comp P = P ∧ ‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 P.range ∧\n      ¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 (P.range →L[𝕜] 𝕜)","steps":[{"fragment":"def UnconditionalCorollaryStatement (𝕜 : Type*) [NontriviallyNormedField 𝕜] : Prop :=","syntax":"def names a proposition depending on the field 𝕜. (𝕜 : Type*) specifies the scalar type, the square-bracketed argument requires a nontrivially normed field structure on it, : Prop identifies a proposition, and := begins its body.","math":"For a fixed nontrivially normed scalar field $\\mathbb K$, define the following unconditional-basis statement.","role":"This statement covers both scalar fields. Defining it for general 𝕜 does not prove it for every field. The selected theorems prove it over ℝ and, through ComplexCorollaryStatement, over ℂ."},{"fragment":"∀ ρ : ℝ, 0 < ρ →","syntax":"∀ quantifies over real tolerances ρ, even when the space has complex scalars. The implication assumes positivity and governs the entire remaining conclusion.","math":"For every real $\\rho>0$, the following witnesses exist.","role":"This is the opening quantifier of Theorem B for the fixed scalar field. Space and projection can depend on ρ."},{"fragment":"∃ X : BanachModel.{0} 𝕜,","syntax":"∃ chooses a BanachModel over the same field 𝕜. That structure specifies a normed vector space and completeness; .{0} concerns the type universe, not finite dimensionality.","math":"There exists a Banach space $X$ over $\\mathbb K$.","role":"This is the ambient-space witness of Theorem B. X.Carrier denotes this chosen space in the later clauses.","terms":["BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 X.Carrier ∧","syntax":"Apply the project basis-existence predicate to the chosen field and space. ∧ means that this and the subsequent clauses all hold for the same witness.","math":"$$X$ has an $\\mathbb N$-indexed $1$-unconditional Schauder basis. Every finite coordinate multiplier with $\\mathbb K$-valued coefficients of modulus at most one is contractive.","role":"This gives the ambient basis. For 𝕜 = ℂ it includes all complex phases, not merely real signs; for 𝕜 = ℝ it uses real multipliers.","terms":["HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis","IsOneUnconditional","UnconditionalSchauderBasis"]},{"fragment":"∃ P : X.Carrier →L[𝕜] X.Carrier,","syntax":"∃ introduces P and →L[𝕜] makes it a continuous 𝕜-linear map from the chosen space to itself.","math":"There exists a bounded $\\mathbb K$-linear operator $P:X\\to X$.","role":"This is the single operator for all remaining requirements. In the complex case it is complex-linear, not only real-linear.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap","BanachModel"]},{"fragment":"P.comp P = P ∧","syntax":"P.comp P composes the operator with itself. Equality to P requires idempotence, and ∧ continues the joint requirements.","math":"$$P^2=P$, so $P$ is a projection.","role":"This is “a projection” in Theorem B, for the fixed field.","terms":["ContinuousLinearMap"]},{"fragment":"‖P‖ < 1 + ρ ∧","syntax":"The norm bars denote the operator norm, < is strict, and ρ is the real tolerance fixed above.","math":"$$\\|P\\|<1+\\rho$.","role":"This is the near-one upper bound in Theorem B. No strict lower bound 1 < ||P|| or complementary projection bound is a clause of this proposition."},{"fragment":"¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 P.range ∧","syntax":"¬ negates existence of a basis. P.range is the range of the chosen 𝕜-linear map, with its inherited norm; the basis predicate uses this same scalar field.","math":"$$Z=P(X)$ admits no unconditional Schauder basis over $\\mathbb K$, finite or indexed by $\\mathbb N$.","role":"This is the first exclusion in Theorem B. The finite case, including the empty basis of the zero space, is included in the negated predicate.","terms":["HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis","Submodule"]},{"fragment":"¬ HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis 𝕜 (P.range →L[𝕜] 𝕜)","syntax":"The parenthesized type is the space of continuous 𝕜-linear maps from P.range to 𝕜. The outer predicate is negated, so this dual space has no basis of either permitted index type.","math":"The continuous dual $Z^*=\\mathcal B_{\\mathbb K}(Z,\\mathbb K)$, with its operator norm, admits no unconditional Schauder basis over $\\mathbb K$.","role":"This completes the basis conclusion for both Z and its continuous dual. With 𝕜 = ℂ the functionals are complex-linear. This template adds no nonlattice or GL/DPR conclusion.","terms":["HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis","ContinuousLinearMap"]}],"children":"$undefined"}]
17:["$","div",null,{"className":"result-grid scalar-pair","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Complex specialisation"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Substitute ℂ for the field in the complete proposition just above. This definition adds no new hypotheses or conclusions; the separate theorem below proves that specialisation."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"data-statement":"ComplexCorollaryStatement","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"def ComplexCorollaryStatement : Prop := UnconditionalCorollaryStatement ℂ","label":"Exact proposition definition · complete body"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"source-line","children":["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/CorollaryStatement.lean","line":92,"children":"Statement source"}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"id":"complexCorollary","className":"proved-reference","children":[["$","$Lf",null,{"code":"theorem complexCorollary : ComplexCorollaryStatement","label":"Proved declaration · proof body omitted"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"verification-line","children":["Verified in Lean ·"," ",["$","$L10",null,{"file":"ComplementedSubspace/ComplexCorollary.lean","line":18,"children":"Source"}]," ","· ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/verification/","children":"Verification details"}]]}]]}]]}]]}]
18:["$","$L11",null,{"guideId":"ComplexCorollaryStatement","code":"def ComplexCorollaryStatement : Prop := UnconditionalCorollaryStatement ℂ","steps":[{"fragment":"def ComplexCorollaryStatement : Prop :=","syntax":"def introduces a named proposition. : Prop gives its kind and := introduces what it means.","math":"We name the complex-field statement ComplexCorollaryStatement.","role":"The closed theorem complexCorollary proves this proposition. The definition itself is not a proof."},{"fragment":"UnconditionalCorollaryStatement ℂ","syntax":"Set 𝕜 = ℂ in the shared proposition. This defines the complex case without adding any assumptions.","math":"For every real $\\rho>0$, there exist a complex Banach space $X$ with a $1$-unconditional Schauder basis and a complex-linear projection $P:X\\to X$ with $\\|P\\|<1+\\rho$, such that neither $Z=P(X)$ nor its continuous complex dual $Z^*$ admits an unconditional Schauder basis.","role":"This is the complex case of Theorem B. Read the full UnconditionalCorollaryStatement body with 𝕜 = ℂ beside this alias: its quantifiers, conjunctions and both negations all remain in force. Ambient multipliers include complex phases; basis exclusions include finite bases. No complex GL/DPR or complex nonlattice conclusion is added.","terms":["UnconditionalCorollaryStatement","HasOneUnconditionalSchauderBasis","HasUnconditionalSchauderBasis","ContinuousLinearMap"]}],"children":"$undefined"}]
